OpenBOR Android Standalone Game Guide
=====================================

OpenBOR games ship as standalone Android applications. One APK contains one
OpenBOR engine build and one game PAK. Players launch the game by its own title
and icon; no PAK picker or shared OpenBOR installation is involved.

Every game needs a permanent Android application ID and release signing key.
Those two values give Android updates their identity. Distinct IDs also let
several OpenBOR games remain installed together with separate PAKs, saves,
logs, and screenshots.


Windows quick start
-------------------

One-time requirements:

- JDK 17
- Android Studio or the Android SDK command-line tools
- Roughly 4 GB of free build space, plus the size of the game PAK

Double-click build.bat after installing the requirements. The builder locates
normal JDK and Android SDK installations automatically. Missing Android SDK
Platform 35, Build Tools 36.0.0, or NDK 21.4.7075529 components can be installed
through the same menu when sdkmanager is available.

First game setup:

1. Put the game PAK beside build.bat and name the source file game.pak.
2. Run build.bat and choose "Edit game settings."
3. Replace the example values, then save and close Notepad.
4. Replace the launcher artwork and optional loading background.
5. Choose "Build a test APK."
6. Test the game, then choose "Build a signed release APK."

The release choice creates a permanent release key automatically when none is
configured. Finished and verified packages appear in engine/android/output.
No PowerShell commands, Gradle commands, PAK copying, APK inspection, or manual
signing steps are required.


Game settings
-------------

The builder creates game.properties from game.properties.example. Six settings
describe the standalone package:

    game.applicationId=io.github.creator.game
    game.name=Game Title
    game.versionCode=1
    game.versionName=1.0.0
    game.pak=game.pak
    game.pakName=game.pak

game.applicationId
    Permanent Android identity for this game. Use two or more lowercase
    segments, normally a reversed domain you control. Creators without a domain
    can use a stable form such as io.github.account.gametitle. Never reuse one
    ID for unrelated games, and never change the ID after publishing.

game.name
    Title displayed by Android.

game.versionCode
    Positive whole number used by Android to order releases. Increase it for
    every published update. The launcher also uses this value to refresh the
    installed PAK without changing its stable name.

game.versionName
    Player-facing release label, such as 1.0.0.

game.pak
    Source PAK path. Keeping game.pak beside build.bat is simplest. Relative
    paths start in engine/android; absolute paths also work. Use forward slashes
    in Windows paths, for example C:/Games/MyGame.pak.

game.pakName
    Stable name created in the installed application's Paks directory. Keep it
    unchanged across updates because OpenBOR derives save and configuration
    names from the PAK name. Use the name expected by any module script that
    inspects its archive, such as SORX.pak or 1.0.0.pak.

The engine Java namespace remains org.openbor.engine. Change only
game.applicationId. Renaming the source namespace breaks the Java package and
does not provide more installation isolation.


Branding
--------

Replace the launcher artwork under app/src/main/res/mipmap-* while preserving
the existing resource names. Each density folder contains the background,
foreground, monochrome, and round icon resources used by Android launchers.

Replace app/src/main/res/drawable/background.png to customize the loading
screen. game.name supplies the application label.

Automated icon generation is intentionally outside the first packaging helper.
Android adaptive icons contain separate foreground, background, monochrome, and
safe-area decisions, so silently resizing one flat image would often produce a
poor production icon.


Test packages
-------------

Choose "Build a test APK" from build.bat. The builder performs these actions:

1. Validates game.properties and the source PAK.
2. Regenerates OpenBOR engine version metadata.
3. Rebuilds the native engine and Android application.
4. Confirms assets/bor.pak is stored without compression.
5. Compares the source and packaged PAK size and SHA-256 hash.
6. Places Game-Title-1.0.0-debug.apk in the output folder.
7. Offers to install it when adb and a connected device are available.

Test first launch, later launches, save creation, controller input, focus
changes, level transitions, and installation over the previous test build.


Release signing and production packages
---------------------------------------

Choose "Build a signed release APK" when testing is complete. The first release
build creates these ignored local files:

    release-key.jks
    keystore.properties

The generated key uses RSA 4096, a random 256-bit password, and a 100-year
validity period. Both files must be backed up together in secure, separate
locations. Losing a self-managed signing key can permanently prevent future
updates outside Google Play App Signing.

Release packaging validates the game identity, version, PAK, signing settings,
keystore, and certificate choice before compilation. The completed signed APK
is verified against the source PAK and copied to the output folder as:

    Game-Title-1.0.0.apk

Never commit release-key.jks, keystore.properties, or their passwords. Existing
ignore rules exclude those files.


Publishing an update
--------------------

Every update must preserve three values:

- game.applicationId
- game.pakName
- release-key.jks and its matching keystore.properties

Increase game.versionCode for every published update and set the desired
game.versionName. Replace game.pak, run the release build again, then install or
publish the verified APK from the output folder.

Changing the application ID produces a different application. Matching IDs
signed with different keys cannot update each other. Matching identity, key,
and a higher version code produce a normal in-place update.

Google Play distribution can use bundleRelease and Play App Signing. Keep the
generated key even when Google Play treats it as an upload key for later
releases.


Linux and macOS
---------------

JDK 17 and the required Android SDK packages must already be installed. Run one
of these commands from engine/android:

    ./build.sh debug
    ./build.sh release
    ./build.sh key

The release action creates the key when necessary and writes the same verified
artifacts to engine/android/output.


Direct Gradle tasks
-------------------

Build automation can invoke the underlying tasks without the helpers:

    gradlew.bat clean packageStandaloneDebug
    gradlew.bat createReleaseKey
    gradlew.bat clean packageStandaloneRelease

Standard assembleDebug, assembleRelease, and bundleRelease tasks remain
available for engine developers and custom release systems.


Runtime behavior
----------------

The launcher copies the bundled PAK on a background thread into Android
app-specific external storage. Copying uses a 64 KiB buffer, flushes and
synchronizes the completed file, validates its size, then publishes it with a
same-directory rename. The engine starts only after the stream closes.

Only LauncherActivity is exported to other applications. GameActivity is
private and starts after the launcher prepares game data. No broad storage
permission is requested.

Backup rules preserve normal user data such as Saves while excluding
regenerable or oversized Paks, Logs, and ScreenShots directories. Uninstalling
an application can still remove its app-specific files.


Troubleshooting
---------------

"JDK 17 was not found"
    Install Eclipse Temurin JDK 17 or select JDK 17 for JAVA_HOME. Later JDK
    releases are not substituted automatically because this build is pinned to
    the JDK version required by its Android Gradle Plugin.

"Android SDK was not found"
    Install Android Studio or the Android command-line tools. The builder checks
    ANDROID_SDK_ROOT, ANDROID_HOME, the normal Android Studio location, and
    C:/android/sdk.

"Game PAK not found or empty"
    Put game.pak beside build.bat or correct game.pak in game.properties.

Android refuses to install an update
    Confirm the application ID and release key match the installed version,
    then increase game.versionCode.

Old game data still launches during local testing
    Increase game.versionCode and rebuild. The installed PAK name remains stable
    while the version code controls replacement.

First launch takes time
    First launch copies the bundled PAK into the engine filesystem. Later
    launches reuse the verified installed copy for that version code.


